# Narrative-judge calibration set (P7-T3b manual Gemini eval).
#
# Each row pairs an expense-report NARRATIVE with the SOURCE FACTS it was meant to
# summarize, plus two HUMAN labels:
#   human_faithful : true  = every claim in the narrative is supported by `facts`
#                            (no invented amounts / vendors / policy outcomes);
#                    false = the narrative contains at least one unsupported or
#                            contradicted claim (a hallucination).
#   human_relevant : true  = the narrative is on-topic — it actually summarizes
#                            THIS expense report (its items, totals, flags);
#                    false = on-topic content is missing / it answers a different
#                            question / it is generic filler.
# faithfulness and relevance are labeled SEPARATELY and on purpose disagree on a
# few rows (a fluent, on-topic narrative can still hallucinate a number; a strictly
# true narrative can still be useless filler) so the judge must discriminate the
# two axes rather than collapse them into one "looks good" score.
#
# The manual judge (real Gemini) is asked the SAME two yes/no questions; Cohen's
# kappa between these human labels and the judge's labels (faithfulness and
# relevance computed separately) calibrates whether the judge can be trusted.
# Mix: clearly faithful+relevant, hallucinated (unfaithful), off-topic (irrelevant),
# and the two cross cases — so neither a constant "yes" nor a constant "no" judge
# scores well.
